OSKit Arch — aarch64 CPU Primitives
===================================

Architecture-specific CPU operations for aarch64: interrupt
enable/disable, context frame construction, syscall-return ABI.
Mirrors the shape of `oskit/arch/x86_64/cpu.lsysl` and
`oskit/arch/trisc/cpu.lsysl` so the kernel can import from
`oskit.arch` without caring which target is underneath.

Bare-instruction implementations (cli/sti, drop_to_el0) live in
`cpu_asm.s` so we can emit `msr`/`eret` without a sysl function
prologue. The SVC dispatcher is hand-written in `vectors.s` and
reads the frame layout defined here.

    module oskit.arch

Offset from a saved SSP to the return-value register slot.
On aarch64 the context frame puts x0 at the lowest address, so
the offset is 0. `syscall_return` writes into this slot; the
dispatcher's restore path pops it back into x0 before `eret`.

Frame layout (34 i64 slots = 272 bytes, sp points at x0):
x0 at +0, x1 at +8, ..., x30 (lr) at +240,
SP_EL0 at +248, ELR_EL1 at +256, SPSR_EL1 at +264.

    val SYSCALL_RETURN_OFFSET = 0
    val STACK_FRAME_SIZE = 272       // 34 fields × 8 bytes

Mask async exceptions (IRQ, FIQ, SError). Runs one instruction
plus the return; safe to call with interrupts already masked.

    extern arch_cli()

Unmask async exceptions.

    extern arch_sti()

Transition from EL1 to EL0 and start executing at `entry` with
`SP_EL0 = sp`. Does not return — an exception back to EL1 is the
way out. Caller must ensure the active page table maps both the
entry point and the user stack EL0-accessible.

    extern drop_to_el0(entry: i64, sp: i64)

Per-thread kernel stack top. Updated by `tss_set_rsp0` on context
switch for API parity with x86/TRISC. On aarch64 the dispatcher
uses whatever `SP_EL1` is live at trap time — SP_EL1 naturally
ends up at the thread's kstack_top after the previous eret pops
the context frame, so this var is informational today but kept
so the scheduler interface stays target-neutral.

    var current_kstack_top: i64 = 0

    tss_set_rsp0(rsp0: i64)
        current_kstack_top = rsp0

Trampoline a thread returns to when its entry function falls off
the end. Issues SYS_EXIT (SLIX native syscall 3) with status 0.
Defined in `board/virt/boot.s`.

    extern thread_exit: byte

Build a fake context frame at the top of a supervisor stack so
the dispatcher's restore path can pop it and `eret` into the
new thread. The frame matches the layout above (x0..x30, SP_EL0,
ELR_EL1, SPSR_EL1). x30 (lr) is primed to `thread_exit` so a
function that returns normally enters the exit path.

SPSR_EL1 = 0 means: M[3:0]=EL0t (AArch64 EL0, SP=SP_EL0), DAIF
unmasked (interrupts will fire after eret).

    build_stack_frame(entry_point: i64, usp: i64, ssp: i64) -> i64
        val sp = ssp - STACK_FRAME_SIZE
        var i = 0
        while i < 31
            *(*i64(sp + i64(i * 8))) = 0
            i += 1
        *(*i64(sp + 240)) = i64(&thread_exit)   // x30 = lr
        *(*i64(sp + 248)) = usp                 // SP_EL0
        *(*i64(sp + 256)) = entry_point         // ELR_EL1
        *(*i64(sp + 264)) = 0                   // SPSR_EL1: EL0t, DAIF=0
        sp

Aarch64 has no ring-0/ring-3 split at the frame level (EL0 is the
user mode and is encoded by SPSR_EL1.M). Alias for parity with
x86_64/TRISC callers.

    build_user_stack_frame(entry_point: i64, usp: i64, ssp: i64) -> i64 =
        build_stack_frame(entry_point, usp, ssp)

Set up the initial context frame for a new process. On aarch64
the per-process kernel stack sits in the shared kernel mapping
(every process's page table clones the kernel's L1 blocks in
`vm_create_process_pt`), so we can build the frame directly
without routing through `vm_copy_to`. Returns the SSP value for
the new thread.

    arch_setup_process_frame(entry_point: i64, usp: i64, ssp: i64, kstack_top_val: i64, ptbr_val: u64) -> i64
        build_stack_frame(entry_point, usp, kstack_top_val)
        kstack_top_val - STACK_FRAME_SIZE
